WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.540
MONOPOLIES DON’T LAST:
Monopolies don’t last. 1904:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:11.580
Rockefeller’s Standard Oil controls 91% of the US&nbsp;
Oil Market. 2004: Standard Oil is just something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:17.760
that we read about in the history books.&nbsp;
2000: GE becomes the world’s largest company.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:25.200
2021: GE announces that they’ll be&nbsp;
divesting into 3 different companies. 2007:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:34.020
Nokia is synonymous with cell phones. 2023: Nokia&nbsp;
is the legacy company with the iconic ringtone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:38.700
2015: Apple, Google, Amazon,&nbsp;
and Meta rule the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:48.180
2045? 2060? When is their expiration? These&nbsp;
companies may seem unstoppable, unbreakable today&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:55.020
but there’s only one thing for certain, each and&nbsp;
every one of these companies will fail. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.020 --> 00:01:00.300
the bigger they get, the faster they’ll&nbsp;
fall. You probabaly asking how. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:07.020
no ones ever gonna stop using smart phones or the&nbsp;
internet right? Well, that’s just because we don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:13.080
yet know what’s next. Henry Ford once famously&nbsp;
said “If I had asked people what they wanted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:20.160
they would have said faster horses.” Today,&nbsp;
horse drawn carriages are virtually obsolete. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:26.160
here’s why all monopolies are doomed to fail and&nbsp;
why big tech has already started their demise.

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:35.640
PHASE 1: DYING FLAME:
People love to hate on monopolies but they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:41.340
often forget that it wasn’t that long ago that&nbsp;
the very same companies were cherished. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:46.080
companies don’t just randomly become monopolies&nbsp;
because they had better marketing or something.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:51.000
Companies become monopolies because they&nbsp;
offered something that was undeniably better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:57.240
than everything else. Standard Oil offered an&nbsp;
undeniably better way for energy production and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:03.840
transportation. AT&amp;T offered an undeniably better&nbsp;
way to communicate. And modern tech companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:09.780
started off by offering an undeniably better way&nbsp;
to navigate the internet. Amazon mastered online&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:15.780
shopping, Apple mastered handheld devices, Google&nbsp;
mastered advertising, and Facebook mastered online&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:22.740
communication. So, ironically, the first stage&nbsp;
of a monopoly is actually love. All monopolies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:29.520
were loved by most of its user base at some point&nbsp;
in time. That’s how they became a monopoly. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:35.520
this love also gives companies a lot of control,&nbsp;
allowing them to do things that most companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:40.200
wouldn’t be able to get away with. Apple, for&nbsp;
example, was able to drop the headphone jack,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:46.320
prevent consumers from repairing their devices,&nbsp;
and even remove the power brick. Yet, they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:52.080
still the biggest company in the world because&nbsp;
people love iPhones. Such anti consumer moves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:57.360
are usually what raise the first red flags that&nbsp;
a given company is actually just a monopoly. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:02.520
by this point, the company already has control&nbsp;
over the market and they shift their focus to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:07.620
building an empire. During this stage, companies&nbsp;
can basically throw out anything they want and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:13.980
it’ll stick. Likely the best example of this was&nbsp;
Microsoft back in the 90s and 2000s. Windows and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:19.260
Office were actually phenomenal products that&nbsp;
people loved. Internet Explorer on the other hand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:26.040
was not but that didn’t matter because Microsoft&nbsp;
was able to push IE using Windows. The same thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:31.560
could be said about Hotmail, Outlook, and the&nbsp;
MSN suite of products. The same thing could be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:36.660
said about Google and Apple throughout the 2010s.&nbsp;
People started using Google Drive because they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:41.820
already had Gmail accounts. People started using&nbsp;
Google Photoes because they were already using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:47.460
Android. Similarly with Apple, people started&nbsp;
using iCloud, Apple Music, Safari, and Apple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:53.460
Maps because it came along with iPhones. This&nbsp;
isn’t to say that these products were bad, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:59.100
they weren’t especially stellar either. I mean,&nbsp;
let’s just be honest, who’s gonna use Apple Maps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:06.660
or Safari or the Apple Mail Client if it wasn’t&nbsp;
for iPhones. The answer is no one and this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:12.120
really the trademark of an aged monopoly. People&nbsp;
use their newer products not because they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:17.310
extraordinary but because they were associated&nbsp;
with something that was extraordinary. Piggy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.310 --> 00:04:22.560
backing mediocre products off of extraordinary&nbsp;
products only works for so long though.

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:28.020
PHASE 2: INEFFICIENCY BUILDS:&nbsp;

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:35.100
The beginning of the end for most monopolies&nbsp;
is actually just themselves. As monopolies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:39.240
grow more powerful, we start to see a clear&nbsp;
disconnect between the company’s original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:45.000
purpose and vision and the goals and vision of the&nbsp;
company's employees. I mean just think about it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.080
what type of person joins a monopoly? Well, it’s&nbsp;
usually someone with the mindset of “If you can’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:57.720
beat em, you might as well join em.” And I&nbsp;
don’t blame these people whatsoever. They’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.720 --> 00:05:02.940
simply looking out for themselves as they should&nbsp;
be, but this doesn’t exactly play out well for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:08.760
the organization. Suddenly, the primary goal of&nbsp;
the average employee is no longer to serve the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:13.800
customer in the best way possible. In fact, the&nbsp;
average employee might not even know how their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:18.660
role impacts the customer as there’s simply so&nbsp;
many layers between them and the final product.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:24.240
So, the primary goal of employees transitions to&nbsp;
impressing their managers, getting promotions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:30.420
and scoring bonuses. This is how it becomes across&nbsp;
the entire corporate ladder. And as for the top,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:36.060
well the top isn’t any better. Most of the&nbsp;
visionary executives have likely already retired,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:41.400
moved onto something else, or have just passed&nbsp;
away. So, the people who left aren’t visionaries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:46.140
they’re the ones that were able to play the&nbsp;
corporate game the best. They were able to handle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:50.880
office politics, and backstabbling, and crappy&nbsp;
managers, and ineffective teams and whatever else.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:57.120
These people aren’t exactly leaders. A better&nbsp;
description would be survivors similar to winning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:01.620
the Hunger Games or something. And given that&nbsp;
this is what they’ve been used to their entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:07.320
career, as executives, they’re gonna focus on&nbsp;
survival. How do they survive at the top? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:12.660
it’s not by serving the customers, it’s by&nbsp;
serving the shareholders. The top two things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:18.180
that shareholders are looking for is growth and&nbsp;
profit and thus the priorities of the company also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:24.600
becomes growth and profit. And what’s the easiest&nbsp;
ways to do this? Well, the easiest ways are to cut&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:30.900
costs and increase prices. A perfect example&nbsp;
of this is Google. With Larry Page and Sergey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.900 --> 00:06:36.180
Brin and the helm, the focus of the company was&nbsp;
to push the boundaries and experiment. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:41.220
Google was always super proud of all their&nbsp;
failures of which they had hundreds. Their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:47.280
philosophy was always you’re gonna have to fail&nbsp;
a bunch before you succeed. But, I’m not sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:51.840
if we can say the same thing about Sundar’s&nbsp;
Google. Over the past several months itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:57.120
Google has been pulling back on Moonshot&nbsp;
ideas substantially. In fact, in some areas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:01.440
they’ve cancelled as many as half of their&nbsp;
projects. It’s easy to say they’re pulling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:07.560
back because of the recession but it’s not like&nbsp;
Google or Facebook or really any big tech company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:13.440
that’s doing layoffs are actually at risk. They’re&nbsp;
not even remotely close to being unprofitable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:18.720
For giants, recessions are simply a time to&nbsp;
maximize efficiency so that they can become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:24.093
even more profitable for the next bull run.&nbsp;
But by the time the desire to maximize short&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.093 --> 00:07:29.580
term profits becomes larger than the desire&nbsp;
to create long term change, the end is near.

00:07:29.580 --> 00:07:32.640
PHASE 3: THE WEAK LINK:&nbsp;

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:41.400
Monopoly is really quite a fitting name for&nbsp;
these companies because most modern monopolies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:46.800
are dependent on just 1 product. It might seem&nbsp;
like they’re everywhere with their hundreds of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:52.560
products and services, but really, the base of&nbsp;
the company can usually be traced back to just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:59.040
one product. To this day, Google Search&nbsp;
accounts for over 50% of all of Google’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:06.420
revenue. iPhones account for over 50% of all of&nbsp;
Apple’s revenue. The only reason Amazon is even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.420 --> 00:08:10.440
profitable is because of AWS. And the entire&nbsp;
business of Meta can be summed up by Facebook,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:16.560
WhatsApp, and Instagram. The only tech giant that&nbsp;
breaks this trend is Microsoft. They’ve become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:21.240
more of a tech holding company than an actual&nbsp;
business which seems to be working out great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.240 --> 00:08:27.000
so far but we’ll have to see how it plays out&nbsp;
over the long term. As for everyone else though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:33.900
their empires are actually extremely fragile. The&nbsp;
reality is that while they may have hundreds of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.900 --> 00:08:39.420
micro services that generate a bunch of revenue,&nbsp;
the only reason that any of these services are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.420 --> 00:08:46.140
successful is because of the base product. iCloud&nbsp;
for example has nearly a billion users but how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.140 --> 00:08:51.840
many people would stick around after the iPhone&nbsp;
is disrupted? Similarly, how many people would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:58.020
even know about the Metaverse if it wasn’t for&nbsp;
Facebook? The reality is that the only reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.020 --> 00:09:03.120
these extensions are successful or even relevant&nbsp;
is because of the success of the original product.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:09.600
Once that fades away, so does the entire company.&nbsp;
This isn’t to say that the company goes bankrupt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:15.780
the day after or something. Rather, they become&nbsp;
a shell of their former self similar to IBM or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.780 --> 00:09:23.340
AT&amp;T or GE. They might be everywhere but at same&nbsp;
time they’re nowhere because the brand no longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.340 --> 00:09:30.300
carries the same weight. Take ChatGPT for example.&nbsp;
Let’s say ChatGPT replaces Google search which it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.300 --> 00:09:36.120
might very well do. As the masses flock over to&nbsp;
ChatGPT, people will naturally start using the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.120 --> 00:09:40.860
products that are associated with ChatGPT vs&nbsp;
the products that are associated with Google.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.860 --> 00:09:46.140
Given that Microsoft is a major investor in&nbsp;
ChatGPT, this means that a lot more people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.140 --> 00:09:52.680
will start using Edge, Bing, Outlook, OneDrive,&nbsp;
and Microsoft Office. Suddenly, Google’s major&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.680 --> 00:09:58.800
product categories of Chrome, Gmail, Google Drive,&nbsp;
and Google Office aren’t nearly as popular despite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:04.440
nothing changing about them. The bottomline is&nbsp;
that the hero product changed and with that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.440 --> 00:10:09.060
all of the auxiliary products also changed.&nbsp;
So, as you can see, most of these big tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.060 --> 00:10:14.400
companies aren’t as invincible as they may&nbsp;
seem. While they may have hundreds or even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:19.080
thousands of popular services, it’s only a&nbsp;
handful of products that really drive people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.080 --> 00:10:24.360
to the ecosystem. Once those products fall&nbsp;
out of favor, so those the entire company.

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:33.300
THE STATE OF FAANG:
Looking at FAANG today, it’s clear that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.300 --> 00:10:39.120
they’re well past stage 1 of being a monopoly.&nbsp;
The days of people loving them were 15 to 20 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.120 --> 00:10:46.260
ago. Nowadays, the sentiment that dominates is&nbsp;
actually concerns. Concerns about their power and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.260 --> 00:10:51.540
influence, concerns about privacy, concerns about&nbsp;
anti consumer behavior. Most of these guys are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.540 --> 00:10:56.760
also well past stage 2 as well. They’ve already&nbsp;
leveraged their marketshare to push other products&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.760 --> 00:11:02.460
and services and become omnipresent. Their focus&nbsp;
is no longer to improve the world but to maximize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.460 --> 00:11:09.240
stock growth. This is why they spend so much money&nbsp;
on stock buybacks. Apple for example has spent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.240 --> 00:11:15.960
$573 billion on buybacks over the past 10 years.&nbsp;
I’ve said this before but that’s literally enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.960 --> 00:11:21.540
money to buyout another fortune 10 company. Such&nbsp;
behavior is only evident in a late stage company.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.540 --> 00:11:29.100
If this was 2007, Apple would’ve been way too busy&nbsp;
with scaling manufacturing, logistics, and R&amp;D to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.100 --> 00:11:36.180
be spending half a trillion on buybacks. But, this&nbsp;
all brings up the question: how much time to these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.180 --> 00:11:42.720
companies have left? Well, for that, we can just&nbsp;
take it from the horse’s mouth itself: Jeff Bezos.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.520 --> 00:12:14.100
Looking around, big tech companies are rapidly&nbsp;
approaching this milestone. Amazon is 29 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.100 --> 00:12:21.060
old. Google is 25 years old. Facebook is 19 years&nbsp;
old. And Apple is 47 years old. But, they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.060 --> 00:12:26.580
completely reinvented with the iPod and iPhone&nbsp;
so the modern Apple is more like 20 years old.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.580 --> 00:12:31.140
So, it’s likely that the utter dominance&nbsp;
of these companies is already behind us.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.260 --> 00:12:38.940
Again, this isn’t to say that these&nbsp;
companies will go bankrupt tomorrow. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.560 --> 00:12:46.500
they have already started transition into becoming&nbsp;
the Ciscos and Intels of the world. And once they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.500 --> 00:12:52.260
spend a good amount of time there, they’ll&nbsp;
become the Xeroxs and Kodaks of the world and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.260 --> 00:12:59.220
that is the inevitable future of FAANG. Clearly,&nbsp;
things aren’t looking too great for big tech but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.220 --> 00:13:04.260
likely the one exception is Microsoft. If&nbsp;
you’re wondering why, check out this video.

